<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Defiance Campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1952">1952</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws was a campaign against apartheid, presented by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in December 1951 and launched on 26 June 1952. It was the first large-scale, multi-racial political mobilisation against apartheid laws under a common leadership: volunteers burned their pass books, entered places reserved for whites and refused to defend themselves in court. Over 2,000 people were arrested in October 1952 alone, and on 9 November police in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> killed 14 protesters. The government answered with stiff penalties and the Public Safety Act, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli">Albert Luthuli</a> called the campaign off in April 1953.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> won the South African election of 1948 and began to impose apartheid measures against Black people, Indians and people of mixed race, restricting political power to white people and allotting areas of the country to different races. In December 1949 leaders of the African National Congress Youth League, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, Walter Sisulu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-tambo">Oliver Tambo</a>, took power in the ANC, which on 17 December adopted a Programme of Action calling for a more militant approach.</p><p>From 1950 the ANC, working in partnership with the South African Indian Congress, promoted demonstrations, mass action, boycotts, strikes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a>, and 8,000 Black people were arrested for defying apartheid laws. The Population Registration Act classified each person permanently by race, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a> decided where they might live. On 26 June 1950 a National Day of Protest asked people to stay away from work; many lost their jobs, and the ANC set up a fund to help them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The launch date of 26 June 1952 became a yearly National Day of Protest and Mourning. The police had been alerted and were armed and prepared. Protesters, many wearing armbands in the ANC's three colours, stayed largely non-violent; those arrested often refused fines, and it was hoped that mass imprisonment would overwhelm the government. In Port Elizabeth on 26 July 1952 Florence Matomela, one of the first women arrested, joined Govan Mbeki, Raymond Mhlaba and Vuyisile Mini in defying the pass laws at New Brighton Railway Station.</p><p>The government labelled the protests anarchy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and disorder, and police often used batons. Charles Swart, the Minister of Justice, issued orders to shoot demonstrators on sight, and the offices of the ANC and the SAIC were raided in July 1952. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> 39 people were injured as well as the 14 killed. The arrests of peaceful protesters disgusted a section of white opinion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The campaign did not reach its goals, but it showed large and growing opposition to apartheid, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> took note, calling the policy a threat to peace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli">Luthuli</a> ended it so that the resistance groups could reorganise in the new political climate. He was later tried for treason, assaulted and deposed as chief of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> clan, and Mandela took over the ANC after him. The campaigns, with bus boycotts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, inspired civil rights activists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Defiance Campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in 1923, was founded at Bloemfontein in 1912; Clements Kadalie’s Industrial and Commercial Workers’ Union organised tens of thousands of workers in the 1920s; and in the 1950s the ANC and its allies ran the <b>Defiance Campaign</b> of 1952 and adopted the Freedom Charter at Kliptown in 1955.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1952 the ANC and the South African Indian Congress launched the <b>Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws</b>: volunteers deliberately broke segregation laws, entering “whites only” facilities and defying curfews, and more than 8,000 were arrested.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
